Description: NVIDIA install path for Oaxaca (detector and early KMS)
 Oaxaca does not ship the NVIDIA driver in its images. This package
 ships what makes installing it on the target machine a single command, and
 what Ubuntu's driver packages leave out for a Wayland desktop:
 .
  * oaxaca-drivers-install: runs ubuntu-drivers install --no-oem with
    the newest NVIDIA open branch that has Canonical's prebuilt signed
    modules (linux-modules-nvidia-<branch>-open-*) for the installed
    kernel, so Secure Boot needs no MOK enrollment. ubuntu-drivers' own
    choice would be the long-term branch (LTSB) over the newer one (NFB).
    With no NVIDIA GPU, or one the open modules do not support,
    ubuntu-drivers' own choice applies. The installed packages are
    recorded in /var/log/oaxaca/drivers.list.
  * an initramfs-tools hook that copies the nvidia, nvidia_modeset,
    nvidia_uvm and nvidia_drm modules into the initramfs once they are
    installed, so the kernel sets the display mode early (KMS) with the
    modeset=1 option that nvidia-kernel-common-* ships. Ubuntu's own hook
    only looks in the DKMS module directory and so skips the prebuilt
    modules. Without the driver the hook copies nothing.
  * nvidia-prime as a dependency: after installing an NVIDIA driver,
    ubuntu-drivers runs "prime-select on-demand" when that command exists,
    which puts a hybrid laptop's NVIDIA GPU into runtime power management
    (gpu-manager then writes /lib/modprobe.d/nvidia-runtimepm.conf). The
    driver packages only recommend nvidia-prime, and Oaxaca installs
    no Recommends, so the dependency is explicit here.
Homepage: https://github.com/santisbon/oaxaca

